# SRG-APP-000850-WSR-000230  -  CAT II
# The web server must, for password-based authentication, store passwords
# using an approved salted key derivation function, preferably using a keyed
# hash.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000850-WSR-000230
group:           V-264349
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-004062
control_rev5:    IA-5

contribution:    For native accounts, Splunk chooses the algorithm that protects stored
                 passwords in $SPLUNK_HOME/etc/passwd through authentication.conf
                 [authentication] passwordHashAlgorithm. SHA512-crypt and SHA256-crypt are
                 salted, iterated crypt derivations (5000 rounds by default, with an
                 optional round count), which is the approved salted key derivation the
                 requirement asks for; MD5-crypt, still in the spec's vocabulary, is not.

governs:         authentication.conf - How users sign in, and the password rules applied to local accounts.

check:           authentication.conf [authentication] passwordHashAlgorithm matches ^SHA(256|512)-crypt(-[1-9][0-9]{3,})?$
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          authentication.conf [authentication] passwordHashAlgorithm https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        A keyed hash (HMAC-based) password store, the requirement's stated
                 preference - not offered by the product; where it is required the
                 credential must be held by the enterprise directory service or identity
                 provider.
external:        File protection for the password store - carried by the operating system's
                 file ownership and permissions on the Splunk installation.
external:        Where authType is LDAP, SAML, or ProxySSO, Splunk stores no password for
                 those users and the storage control rests with that provider.
external:        FIPS validation of the hash implementation - carried by the host
                 platform's cryptographic module (see SRG-APP-000610).

citation:        splunk-spec-files/authentication.conf.spec:58 (passwordHashAlgorithm =
                 [SHA512-crypt|SHA256-crypt|SHA512-crypt-<num_rounds>|SHA256-crypt-<num_rounds>|MD5-crypt],
                 5000 rounds, minimum 1000 rounds, default SHA512-crypt)
finding:         Native account passwords are not stored using an approved salted key
                 derivation function - authentication.conf [authentication]
                 passwordHashAlgorithm is set to MD5-crypt or to a round count below the
                 spec minimum; this determination is from the perspective of the
                 application layer alone, the keyed-hash option the requirement prefers not
                 being offered by the product and the protection of the stored file resting
                 with the host operating system.
